@database "cognitive_stack"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: cognitive_stack.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Cognitive Stack"
@{h1}Cognitive Stack

Working memory as a stack. When an agent has to interrupt what it is doing
-- a failing test in the middle of a release, a question inside a question
-- it pushes the current task onto the stack, works on the interruption,
and pops back to where it was, instead of keeping the nesting in prose and
losing it a few turns later. Each frame holds a short description and,
optionally, some structured data. An optional hook shows the top frames to
the model before each LLM call. The stack lives in memory only -- it is
working notes for a run, not a record. It has no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The stack tool  " link tool}
  @{"  The stack hook  " link hook}
  @{"  Where the state lives  " link state}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    cognitive_stack:
      type: cognitive_stack
      enabled: true
      max_depth: 50
      max_frames_in_prompt: 3
@{body}

An agent sees the tool when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+cognitive_stack/*"
@{body}

The hook is off until an agent switches it on; see
@{" The stack hook " link hook}. No extra packages.

@{h2}Server settings

The settings stand directly in the server entry, as above -- not under a
@{tt}config:@{utt} block, where the plugin does not look. What is not set
takes the default below. They are read once, when the plugin starts.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_depth | 20 | frames one stack may hold; a push beyond it is refused
max_frames_in_prompt | 3 | the hook: frames shown; 0 or less shows all
session_ttl_seconds | 3600 | a stack no tool call touched for this long is removed
@{body}

The ranges in the schema (5 to 100, 1 to 10, 60 to 86400) are not
enforced. The plugin stores nothing on disk; see
@{" Where the state lives " link state}.
@endnode

@node tool "The stack tool"
@{h1}The stack tool

One tool, named like the plugin instance (@{tt}cognitive_stack@{utt} in the
default setup), with five operations chosen by @{tt}operation@{utt}
(@{tt}op@{utt} is accepted too).

@{table}
Operation | Does
push_batch | puts one or more frames on top, in the order given
pop_batch | removes the top frames and returns them, topmost first
peek | returns the top frames without removing them, bottom one first
list | returns all frames, bottom first
clear | removes all frames; the stack itself stays
@{body}

@{h2}Which stack a call works on

@{bullet}Without @{tt}stack_id@{utt}: the stack of this conversation. The
first push creates it with a random 10-character ID; every later call of
the conversation finds it again. Until then, every operation but a push
answers "No active cognitive stack found".
@{bullet}With @{tt}stack_id@{utt}: that stack, whichever conversation made
it. A push to an ID the plugin does not know creates a stack under that ID,
and from then on it is this conversation's stack. The other operations
answer "Stack X not found" for an unknown ID.
@{bullet}A sub-agent runs in a conversation of its own, so it starts its
own stack unless it is given an ID.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
operation | all | push_batch, pop_batch, peek, list or clear
items | push_batch | a list of objects: @{tt}context@{utt} (text, not empty) and optional @{tt}data@{utt} (an object, stored as sent)
count | pop_batch | frames to pop; default 1, must be 1 or more; more than the stack holds pops all
depth | peek | top frames to return; default 1; 0 or less returns all
stack_id | all | see above
@{body}

A number must be whole: 3, 3.0 and "3" are taken, 2.5 and true are
refused. A push is all or nothing: one bad item refuses the whole batch,
and nothing is pushed. So is the depth limit: a batch that would take the
stack past @{tt}max_depth@{utt} is refused as a whole. There is no limit on
the length of a context or the size of the data.

@{h2}Answers

@{table}
Operation | Fields
push_batch | stack_id, pushed_count, frames (frame_id and context of each pushed frame), depth, max_depth, message
pop_batch | stack_id, popped_count, frames, remaining_depth, message
peek | stack_id, frames, total_depth, message; an empty stack: depth 0 and "Stack is empty"
list | stack_id, frames, depth, max_depth, created_at, last_accessed
clear | stack_id, cleared_frames, message
@{body}

Every answer has @{tt}status@{utt}: success or error. A frame in
@{tt}frames@{utt} carries frame_id, context, data and timestamp (the
server's local time), except in the push answer.

A refused call answers @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "..."}@{utt} and
changes nothing. The texts:

@{table}
error | When
Unknown operation: x. Valid: push_batch, pop_batch, peek, list, clear | no or an unknown operation
items array cannot be empty for push_batch. ... | no items (the text shows the right form)
items must be an array | items is not a list
Each item must be an OBJECT with 'context' key, got: str. ... | an item that is not an object
Each item must have non-empty 'context' | a context that is missing, empty or not text
'data' must be an object | data that is not an object
Batch would exceed max depth (50). Current: 49, trying to add: 2 | the depth limit, with a hint to pop or clear first
No active cognitive stack found. Use push_batch first to create a stack. | no stack_id and the conversation has no stack
Stack X not found | an unknown stack_id
Stack is empty | pop_batch on an empty stack, with a hint to push first
count must be at least 1 | count below 1
x must be a whole number, got ... | a count or depth that is not whole
No active stack for this session (provide an explicit stack_id to clear a specific stack) | clear in a conversation that has no stack
@{body}

Anything else that goes wrong answers the same way with the exception
text.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "push_batch", "items": [{"context": "Release 2.4: finish the changelog", "data": {"branch": "release/2.4"}}, {"context": "CI fails on Windows: find out why before going on"}]}
{"operation": "peek"}
{"operation": "pop_batch", "count": 1}
@{body}
@endnode

@node hook "The stack hook"
@{h1}The stack hook

@{tt}inject_stack_context@{utt} runs before each LLM call and shows the
model where it stands on the stack, so it can pop back without asking for
the list. It is off until an agent switches it on.

@{h2}What it appends

A message at the end of the history, from the run (role
@{tt}developer@{utt}, marked with the instance name in
@{tt}injected_by@{utt}, so two instances do not replace each other's
block):

@{bullet}While the conversation has no stack, or an empty one: a short
reminder that the tool exists, with an example push, list and pop.
@{bullet}With frames: the depth and the top @{tt}max_frames_in_prompt@{utt}
frames, topmost first, each context cut at 150 characters and followed by
the names of its data keys (not their values).

@{code markdown}
## Active Cognitive Stack

**Depth**: 2/50

**Working Memory (top frames):**
- **Frame #2**: CI fails on Windows: find out why before going on
- **Frame #1** [data: branch]: Release 2.4: finish the changelog
@{body}

The message is added only when it differs from the last one the hook
added: a step that did not change the stack adds nothing. When compaction
removed the last one, it comes back. Without messages or without a session
the hook does nothing; if it fails, the error is logged and the call goes
on without it. It has 5 seconds.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the agent's YAML; its setting goes in the same entry:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      cognitive_stack.inject_stack_context:
        enabled: true
        max_frames_in_prompt: 1
@{body}

@{h2}Settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_frames_in_prompt | 3 | frames shown; 0 or less: all
@{body}

A key in the agent's override wins; otherwise the key in the plugin's
server entry; otherwise the default above. A value that is not a whole
number makes the hook fail (logged) and add nothing.
@endnode

@node state "Where the state lives"
@{h1}Where the state lives

Everything is held in the memory of the server process that runs the
plugin, and nothing else.

@{bullet}@{b}Per conversation@{ub}: a stack belongs to the conversation
(the chat session) that created it; the hook and a call without
@{tt}stack_id@{utt} find it that way. A conversation has one stack at a
time.
@{bullet}@{b}Per process@{ub}: the store is shared by every agent and user
the process serves. The API and a command-line run are different processes
with different stores; a woken session runs in a new process and finds
nothing of the old one.
@{bullet}@{b}Restart@{ub}: nothing survives. A restart, a reload of the
plugin or a new process starts empty.

@{h2}How big it can grow

@{bullet}A stack holds at most @{tt}max_depth@{utt} frames (20 in code, 50
in the default configuration).
@{bullet}There is no limit on the number of stacks, the length of a context
or the size of a frame's data.
@{bullet}A stack is removed when no tool call touched it for
@{tt}session_ttl_seconds@{utt} (one hour); the hook showing it does not
count. The check runs at the start of each tool call and before the hook
shows the stack, so the prompt never shows a stack the next call finds
gone.
@{bullet}@{tt}clear@{utt} empties a stack but keeps it and the
conversation's link to it.

@{h2}Clearing

In a conversation, @{tt}clear@{utt} without @{tt}stack_id@{utt} empties
the conversation's own stack; with none it is refused -- it never touches
other conversations' stacks. Called outside a conversation (directly from
code, with no session), it removes every stack of the process.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The tool description comes from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}: the
five operations, the item form with an example, and that the stack is
tracked per session, so the model need not keep the stack_id. Refused
calls answer with the error texts in @{" The stack tool " link tool}. The
chat's status line shows "Pushed 2 frames (depth: 2/50)", "Popped 1
frame(s) (depth: 1)", "Peeked top 1 frame(s)", "Listed 2 frame(s)" or
"Cleared 2 frame(s)", and the error text when a call is refused.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are small but not bounded: @{tt}list@{utt} returns every frame with
its full context and data, and a push echoes each pushed context back. Tool
answers are appended like any other; nothing earlier changes. The hook
appends its block at the end of the history and only when it changed; it
never rewrites the system prompt or an earlier message, so the cached
prompt in front of it stays intact. Every changed block stays in the
history, though: each push or pop with the hook on adds one block to the
conversation.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A stack ID is all it takes: any conversation that has one can
read, change or clear that stack.
@{bullet}The server settings are read once; a change takes effect after a
restart.
@endnode
